WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.780
Welcome to another edition
of Dr. Greger in the Kitchen.

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.360
"Dr. Greger in the Kitchen:
Groatnola"

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.420
Today, we're going to
be making “groatnola,”

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.340
which arose out
of my frustration

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:13.620
with finding a healthy
breakfast cereal.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:17.520
This is an SOS-free recipe
with no added salt, oil, or sugar,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.680
which is hard to find
in the grocery store.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.780
There’s two last
time I checked;

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.460
one’s called
Uncle Sam's cereal,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:24.900
which is toasted,

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.480
flattened wheat berries
and flaxseeds.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.880
You get a little box;
it’s expensive.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.700
And then, there's some
brands of muesli

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.200
which don't have
any crap added,

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.546
but I always find muesli
kind of mushy.

00:00:37.918 --> 00:00:40.260
I love the breakfast
cereal from my youth

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:42.858
but just don't want
all that added garbage.

00:00:42.858 --> 00:00:46.456
So, how about groatnola?

00:00:46.456 --> 00:00:47.940
Groats, of course,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.658
starting out with whole,
intact grains,

00:00:50.658 --> 00:00:51.780
not just groats.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.420
Although, we're actually
not using a grain today at all,

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.340
but buckwheat groats.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:57.660
Buckwheat,
despite its name,

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:59.743
has nothing to do with wheat;
it’s not even a grain.

00:00:59.743 --> 00:01:06.211
It's a starchy seed of a non-
grass plant-family...plant.

00:01:06.211 --> 00:01:11.461
So, we're going to start by
making these raw groats.

00:01:11.461 --> 00:01:13.320
We're going to
cook a cup of them.

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.862
If you have an
Instant Pot,

00:01:14.862 --> 00:01:18.300
it’s one cup of
buckwheat groats

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:21.418
to a cup and a half of water,
pressure cooked for five minutes.

00:01:21.418 --> 00:01:22.800
I don’t have an
Instant Pot here,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.419
so we're just going to
cook it on the stove,

00:01:24.419 --> 00:01:26.420
but it only takes
ten minutes.

00:01:26.523 --> 00:01:31.226
Then, we are going to
nuke a sweet potato—

00:01:31.226 --> 00:01:33.480
a nice big, juicy,
round sweet potato.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.620
We're going to microwave it.

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.780
It depends on your microwave…
five minutes to ten minutes

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.331
until it's nice and juicy
and delicious inside.

00:01:40.331 --> 00:01:42.687
And...we'll be right back.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:45.565
And while we wait…

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.360
ah, ten minutes is
too much for burpees.

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.827
So, I’m sorry,
no burpees this time.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.520
And we are back.
Buckwheat is done.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:02.760
Throw that in a
big mixing bowl.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.780
We have our sweet potato.
Look at that beautiful color there.

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.545
Don’t burn yourself.

00:02:08.545 --> 00:02:11.580
And then,
use a potato masher

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.240
or one of those zigzag wire
ones is a little easier,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.940
just to mash this together—

00:02:17.940 --> 00:02:21.803
the cooked buckwheat and
the cooked sweet potato—

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.458
until it all becomes nice
and homogeneous.

00:02:26.751 --> 00:02:29.400
Okay, and then,
we are going to put a teaspoon

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:31.555
of pumpkin pie spice

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:35.000
and a teaspoon of
just plain cinnamon.

00:02:39.138 --> 00:02:40.620
It's already smelling good.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:47.513
And then, we are going to
add 12 cups of rolled oats.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:49.680
If you like big chunks,

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.780
you can add less
of the rolled oats

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:54.660
and if you like it more
like little crunchy bits,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.334
you can add more.

00:02:56.334 --> 00:02:57.957
Now, if you have
dogs at home,

00:02:57.957 --> 00:03:01.800
what I would do is wait to
put the spices in until the end,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.740
and so then, you just
have the three ingredients—

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:07.500
the sweet potato,
oats, and buckwheat,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.660
and you can roll them
into little balls,

00:03:09.660 --> 00:03:12.360
and all four of my
dogs love them.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.700
Unfortunately, I don’t have
any of my dogs here,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.560
so I just added
the spices early,

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.340
which makes it a little
more evenly distributed

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:21.751
throughout the mix.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.560
So, you just want to mix this
until it's down to little clumps.

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:27.360
Now, this is going
to make about

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:32.040
three cookie trays
worth of groatnola.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:33.628
I like making a
whole big batch;

00:03:33.628 --> 00:03:37.153
so, this is one sweet
potato’s worth of groatnola.

00:03:37.153 --> 00:03:40.500
Okay, see, it's this
kind of chunky texture

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.737
that you might see
with regular granola.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.620
Then, take a cookie tray

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:50.740
and put about a
third of it in there,

00:03:51.079 --> 00:03:55.707
and basically, you want
kind of a single layer.

00:03:55.909 --> 00:03:58.116
Kinda smush it down.

00:03:59.297 --> 00:04:02.580
And then, put it in the oven
at a low temperature:

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.060
250°F for two and a half hours.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.180
If you have a convection setting
and can get some air circulating,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.280
that’s even better,
but optional.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.650
I put that in, and I have
a tray that's already done.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.500
Alright, here's the
finished batch.

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.920
As you can see it's all light,
crispy, medium chunky.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.973
It has the sweetness
from the sweet potato;

00:04:21.973 --> 00:04:25.800
it has the intact groats
from the buckwheat,

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:27.358
the oat goodness.

00:04:27.358 --> 00:04:29.520
And then, do whatever
you want to do

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:30.900
in terms of granola.

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:32.400
You can put it on
a smoothie bowl,

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:36.720
sprinkle it on some frozen
banana soft-serve ice cream

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:38.100
that you make in a
blender or something.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:41.400
I just treat it like regular
breakfast cereal.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:44.280
So, I have a groatnola
base with some berries,

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:46.500
some pumpkin seeds,
some walnuts,

00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:48.660
and then, some
cocoa powder,

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:50.220
just because I like
everything chocolatey.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.740
Put some unsweetened
soy milk on there,

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:54.900
and you are good to go.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:58.421
You will have weeks
of groatnola to enjoy.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.920
Would you like
some recipes?

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.580
Go to the
NutritionFacts.org recipe page,

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:06.840
where you will
find recipes

00:05:06.840 --> 00:05:09.420
where not just every
recipe is health-promoting,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.720
but every ingredient of every
recipe is health-promoting—

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:15.720
a green light food,
a whole plant food.

00:05:15.720 --> 00:05:16.736
So, wait a second,

00:05:16.736 --> 00:05:18.300
how do you make stuff
sweet without sugar?

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:19.980
How do you make stuff
salty without salt?

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.560
Those are some of the
challenges that we took on

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:24.240
to bring you
healthy recipes

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:27.227
at the
NutritionFacts.org recipe page.

00:05:30.825 --> 00:05:36.480
Sweet potatoes are one of
my favorite go-to travel snacks.

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:38.460
You will see me
on airplanes

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:41.340
whipping out my
microwaved sweet potato.

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:44.220
They make perfect
hand warmers.

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:48.420
I went to medical
school in chilly Boston.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:49.794
During the winter,

00:05:49.794 --> 00:05:51.360
microwaved two sweet
potatoes in the morning,

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.280
put them in my
jacket pockets

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:55.440
to keep my hands
nice and warm,

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:01.183
and then a perfect,
healthy snack for later on.

00:06:01.183 --> 00:06:03.180
Yeah, I love
sweet potatoes.

